# Declared test toolchain — what CI's `tests` matrix installs.
#
# EDIT THIS FILE, then run `python3 scripts/lock_toolchain.py` to regenerate the
# hash-pinned `test-tools.txt` beside it.
#
# Resolved UNIVERSALLY from the >=3.9 floor, so one file covers the whole
# 3 OS x 3 Python matrix. uv emits environment markers per interpreter, which
# means 3.12/3.14 still get current pytest while 3.9 gets the last release that
# supported it — locking does not drag modern Pythons back to the floor's
# versions. Left unpinned here on purpose: the resolver picks the newest release
# valid for each interpreter, and `lock_toolchain.py` is when that choice gets
# made and reviewed.
pytest
pytest-cov
coverage
hypothesis
# tests/unit/test_sdist_contents.py runs setuptools' own MANIFEST.in matcher
# (`setuptools._distutils.filelist.FileList`) rather than reimplementing it.
# `python -m venv` stopped seeding setuptools in 3.12 and the `tests` job does
# no editable install, so it has to be declared here: locally `make venv` also
# installs release-tools.txt, which pins it, and the test passed on every
# developer machine while every leg of the CI matrix raised ModuleNotFoundError.
setuptools
# tests/unit/test_canary_deps.py reads .github/workflows/*.yml to check that
# every CI job assembling its own venv installs what the suite then imports.
# It opened with `pytest.importorskip("yaml")`, and PyYAML was pinned only in
# lint-tools.txt and mutation-tools.txt — so the file skipped in ALL SIX jobs
# that run the suite, including the required `tests` leg it was written to
# protect. A check that silently skips in every job it guards is not a check;
# `test_every_harness_job_can_import_yaml` now fails the build if that comes
# back.
pyyaml
